    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **261 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-creation rate limit in the Spring PetClinic REST app.\n\n**Change** \u2014 `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner()` (`src/main/java/.../rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`):\n- Added a guard that returns **HTTP 429 (`TOO_MANY_REQUESTS`)** when 100 or more owners already carry today's `registrationDate`.\n- Added a `MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY = 100` constant and a `countOwnersRegisteredToday()` helper that counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals `LocalDate.now()`. The count is evaluated before the new owner is persisted, so the owner being created is not included \u2014 mirroring the existing per-city capacity check's conventions.\n\nThe check is placed right after the existing per-city capacity check and before the household/telephone-dedup logic and persistence.\n\n**Tests** \u2014 `OwnerRestControllerV1Tests.java`:\n- `createOwnerRejectedWhenDailyLimitReached` \u2014 seeds 100 owners registered today, then expects the next POST to return 429.\n- `createOwnerAllowedWhenBelowDailyLimit` \u2014 seeds 99 owners registered today, then expects the next POST to succeed (201).\n- Both use a `seedOwnersRegisteredToday(int)` helper with unique cities/telephones/names so no other validation (per-city cap, telephone/household duplication) interferes.\n\nNote: today's date shown in context is 2026-08-09; seeded PetClinic data has null `registrationDate`, so it never counts toward the daily total.",
